digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a label-free, untrained DENN for single-shot optical phase retrieval through scattering media by embedding an experimentally calibrated empirical transmission matrix as a forward-model constraint in a neural network, especially for systems lacking tractable analytical theory and with applicability across calibrated scattering media.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When supervised or analytical-model-based approaches are limited by data demands or missing analytical theory, an experimentally calibrated empirical forward model can motivate an untrained neural framework for phase retrieval through scattering media."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Since DIP-type untrained phase retrieval typically relies on a forward mapping and the paper integrates an empirical model with a convnet to remove labeled data and training, integrating a calibrated empirical model into an untrained convnet is a plausible design for label-free phase retrieval through scattering systems."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Physics-enhanced DNNs are supported as efficient for single-intensity phase retrieval in settings where phase retardation is precisely described by analytical theory."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The reported simulations and experiments, together with single-shot speckle demonstrations through multimode fiber and ground glass, support feasibility of the DENN approach in the demonstrated single-intensity scattering settings."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The NN+TM method is supported as applicable across calibrated scattering media, while broader extension to other black-box inverse problems is conditional on obtaining a suitable calibrated empirical forward model."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The paper's research proposal is to build and evaluate an untrained DENN framework that integrates a calibrated empirical transmission matrix with a neural network for phase retrieval in scattering systems, with applicability across calibrated media."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Demonstrated DENN results for single-intensity phase retrieval and applicability of the calibrated empirical TM across scattering media support the paper's empirical-model-constrained untrained DENN proposal for scattering-media phase retrieval."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Deep learning is a widely used paradigm for inverse problems, and supervised deep learning can learn unknown system mappings from many paired inputs and outputs."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Because deep learning is widely used for inverse problems and data burden remains a bottleneck, pursuing data-reducing untrained or physics-constrained neural methods is a plausible direction for inverse problems."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) Challenging multimode-fiber inverse problems and the PhysenNet example motivate considering untrained or physics-enhanced strategies for difficult optical inverse problems."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Although the empirical TM is only a partial experimental measurement, its capture of input-output relationships and update insights makes it a plausible constraint for untrained optimization in scattering systems."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Active use of untrained DIP-style approaches together with the calibrated-TM design rationale motivates investigating the DENN research idea."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) The calibrated-TM design rationale and existing physics-enhanced untrained-network precedent make DENN a plausible research direction to investigate for challenging optical inverse problems."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because data requirements remain a bottleneck and complete physical models can reduce large training-set needs when incorporated into DNNs, it is plausible to investigate untrained or physics-constrained neural approaches as a response to data burden."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) An experimentally calibrated empirical transmission matrix integrated into the DNN is positioned as a real-response forward component for enabling backpropagation in the DENN."];
  "c_analytic_limited" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given analytical models mainly apply when strong scattering is absent and multiple scattering is immensely complex, therefore analytical forward models are often unavailable in strong or backscattering media."];
  "c_applicable" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the empirical TM can be calibrated for different scattering media and calibration across media enables transfer without retraining, therefore the NN+TM method is applicable to various scattering media."];
  "c_bridge_to_proposal" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given an empirical differentiable forward model is a plausible replacement for analytical theory and a calibrated empirical TM can plausibly provide sufficient constraint, therefore integrating a calibrated TM into an untrained network is a well-supported design choice."];
  "c_challenge" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given DIP phase retrieval over scattering media is attractive but challenging and analytical-theory-based physics-enhanced DNNs are inapplicable without analytical theory, therefore the key obstacle is the lack of an analytical forward model and the challenge is to supply an alternative constraint."];
  "c_data_gap" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given unsupervised or self-supervised learning still needs large amounts of data and supervised learning also needs large labeled datasets, therefore data burden remains a key bottleneck and motivates reducing or eliminating training data needs."];
  "c_dip_requires_model" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given DIP variants enforce physics consistency and typical DIP-type methods use a forward mapping that is an analytical physical model, therefore DIP-type untrained phase retrieval typically relies on an analytical forward model."];
  "c_dip_uptake" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given DIP-inspired untrained networks are widely used across metrology, holography, and imaging and there is a growing pursuit of untrained physics-enhanced DNNs, therefore untrained DIP-style approaches are a credible and active methodological direction."];
  "c_empirical_candidate" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given analytical forward models are unavailable in strong scattering and differentiable empirical models based on real responses are a promising open attempt, therefore an empirical differentiable forward model is a plausible replacement for analytical theory."];
  "c_inapplicable_gap" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given physics-enhanced DNNs update using a loss based on analytical theory and many optical systems lack analytical theory, therefore analytical-theory-based physics-enhanced DNNs are inapplicable to those systems."];
  "c_no_training" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given integrating the empirical model with a convnet eliminates labeled data and training and data burden motivates eliminating training data, therefore this integration achieves an untrained and label-free objective."];
  "c_practical" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given supervised learning requires large labeled datasets and extensive training, and such requirements make methods impractical when data are scarce or costly, therefore supervised learning is impractical for many inverse problems with limited data."];
  "c_single_shot_support" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given single-shot speckle enables seeing through multimode fiber and ground glass and the DENN aims at single-intensity phase retrieval in scattering, therefore this single-shot result supports the DENN feasibility target."];
  "c_untrained_trend" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given there is a growing pursuit of untrained and physics-enhanced DNNs and data burden motivates alternatives, therefore untrained physics-enhanced DNNs are a reasonable direction to reduce data dependence."];
  "i_general" [label="(0,0,0) If an untrained DNN approach uses a calibrated empirical forward model as its constraint instead of analytical theory, then the same strategy can generalize to other black-box inverse problems where such empirical forward models can be measured."];
  "i_need" [label="(0,0,0) If an inverse-problem method requires large datasets and extensive training, then it may be impractical in scenarios where data acquisition is costly or limited."];
  "i_transfer" [label="(0,0,0) If a forward model component can be experimentally calibrated for each new medium, then a reconstruction method that relies on that component can be transferred to that medium without retraining on large labeled datasets."];
  "n10_s" [label="(10,0,0) Currently physics-enhanced DNNs are efficient for single-intensity phase retrieval when phase retardation is precisely described by theory."];
  "n11_s" [label="(11,0,0) Currently using a differentiable empirical model based on real system response to generate estimates for untrained updates is promising but still an open attempt."];
  "n12_s" [label="(12,0,0) Currently analytical models such as BPM apply mainly when strong scattering is absent and suit weak-scattering with negligible backscattering."];
  "n13_s" [label="(13,0,0) Currently many optical systems lack analytical theory, and wave-scattering systems with non-negligible backward scattering are typical examples."];
  "n15_s" [label="(15,0,0) Currently applying DIP to optical phase retrieval over a scattering medium is attractive but challenging."];
  "n16_s" [label="(16,0,0) Currently the paper reports a DENN to tackle scattering systems without analytical theory, demonstrating phase retrieval through an optical MIMO system in simulation and experiment."];
  "n17_s" [label="(17,0,0) Currently an experimentally calibrated empirical transmission matrix (TM) for the MIMO forward mapping is integrated into the DNN to enable backpropagation."];
  "n18_s" [label="(18,0,0) Currently the empirical TM captures relationships between multiple inputs and corresponding outputs in the scattering system."];
  "n19_s" [label="(19,0,0) Currently unlike analytical theory, the empirical TM is only a partial experimental measurement but still provides insights to update the DENN."];
  "n1_s" [label="(1,0,0) Currently deep learning is widely used as a paradigm to solve inverse problems across many scientific and engineering fields."];
  "n20_s" [label="(20,0,0) Currently integrating the empirical model of the opaque system with a convnet eliminates the need for labeled data and eliminates the need for training."];
  "n21_s" [label="(21,0,0) Currently proof-of-principle seeing through a multimode fiber and ground glass is achieved using single-shot speckle intensity outputs."];
  "n24_s" [label="(24,0,0) Currently simulations and experiments verify feasibility and superiority of the proposed DENN for single-intensity phase retrieval."];
  "n25_s" [label="(25,0,0) Currently because the empirical TM can be calibrated per scattering medium, the NN+TM combination is applicable across various media."];
  "n2_s" [label="(2,0,0) Currently supervised deep learning can learn unknown system mappings end-to-end from many paired inputs and outputs."];
  "n33_r5" [label="(5,32,1) Currently many optical transport and inverse problems through multimode fibers are challenging, and learning-based approaches can be limited by training data constraints."];
  "n33_r6" [label="(7,33,5) If a complete physical model of image formation is incorporated into a DNN, then the need for large training sets can be overcome."];
  "n33_r6b" [label="(10,33,6) Currently PhysenNet is an example of a physics-enhanced DNN that can be used without prior training."];
  "n3_s" [label="(3,0,0) Currently learning such mappings via supervised learning requires large labeled datasets and extensive training, which is time- and energy-intensive."];
  "n4_s" [label="(4,0,0) Currently unsupervised and self-supervised learning reduces pairing and labeling effort, but still needs large amounts of data."];
  "n55_r3" [label="(13,55,3) Currently the multiple-scattering process is immensely complex."];
  "n5_s" [label="(5,0,0) Currently the community is pursuing untrained DNNs, and physics-enhanced DNNs integrate physical priors to reduce reliance on big data."];
  "n6_s" [label="(6,0,0) Currently inspired by deep image prior (DIP), untrained neural networks have been used in metrology, holography, and imaging."];
  "n7_s" [label="(7,0,0) Currently DIP variants exist for optical phase retrieval, and physics consistency is enforced by minimizing loss between estimated results and actual measurements."];
  "n8_s" [label="(8,0,0) Currently typical DIP-type methods generate estimates using a forward mapping function and require an analytical physical model of evolution."];
  "n9_s" [label="(9,0,0) Currently the weights and biases of a physics-enhanced DNN can be updated using a loss function based on the analytical theory."];
  "i_need" -> "c_practical" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n3_s" -> "c_practical" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n4_s" -> "c_data_gap" [label="induction-case"];
  "n3_s" -> "c_data_gap" [label="induction-common"];
  "n7_s" -> "c_dip_requires_model" [label="induction-case"];
  "n8_s" -> "c_dip_requires_model" [label="induction-common"];
  "n12_s" -> "c_analytic_limited" [label="induction-case"];
  "n55_r3" -> "c_analytic_limited" [label="induction-common"];
  "n9_s" -> "c_inapplicable_gap" [label="deduction-rule"];
